Follow-up to aiecompiler_part_{probe,ipuv1cnn}.log, which established that no
guessed --part string derives an architecture. Rather than keep guessing,
looked for the real device database this install ships: RDI_APPROOT/data/parts
(RDI_APPROOT = one level above bin/, resolved by reading aiecompiler.bat and
setupEnv.bat directly rather than assuming). That tree exists and contains
exactly one populated entry:

  data/parts/xilinx/xclbin/strx/base.xclbin

Its embedded build_metadata (read with the same regex byte-scanner used for
the phoenix xclbin previously) gives a real, well-formed part string for
Strix:

  board_id: "strx", part: "xc10AIE2P_ML-die-0x-e-S",
  PlatformVBNV: "amd_strx_strx_202410_1"

Fed --part=xc10AIE2P_ML-die-0x-e-S (a real string pulled from AMD's own
shipped data, not a guess) to the same trivial graph in tools/aie_probe/ that
every prior attempt used. Result below: the "AIE architecture could not be
auto derived" error is GONE. It derives __AIE_ARCH__=21 and proceeds to
invoke Peano's own bundled clang for the ADF frontend's preprocessing pass,
which now fails on something completely different (missing <iostream> --
see aiecompiler_hostlib_missing.log). This is the important result: it
proves the --part mechanism works end-to-end on this install when fed a
real device string. The prior wall was never a broken flag or a broken tool
-- it was that this install's shipped part database has no Phoenix entry,
only Strix's.

